_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Reviewing the chronological command trace: (1) Several iterative playbook-edit/run cycles (cmd-41aa54e55bd64bf3, cmd-43614ed87cd742f4) failed due to a VyOS shell alias-expansion bug with 'set -e' — legitimate troubleshooting iterations toward the final working playbook; the failed attempts left no lingering bad config since VyOS commit failed atomically. (2) A one-off manual (non-idempotent, outside-playbook) sequence of ad-hoc SSH commands (cmd-926ca1b6948a4131, cmd-a249367fec11479e, cmd-ec1a782320e54f0e, cmd-f5481f57f3274e9b, cmd-7799e2b5e5ed410e) was used to diagnose/apply the eth1 address and NAT rule directly via vbash before the alias bug was understood and fixed in the playbook — bounded and low-risk, and reconciled by the final idempotent Ansible run rather than left as drift. (3) A background tcpdump was started on node1 (cmd-d421ddc2da10498a) for diagnosis and was explicitly killed shortly after (cmd-8446b2b324d546a7 includes 'kill %1'), a fully bounded, reverted diagnostic action. (4) The NAT-disable/re-enable test (cmd-68970f92152f4b33 through cmd-46a14016309043e7) was a deliberate, bounded, fully-restored disruption directly required to prove dependency. (5) Temporary test scripts were written to /tmp on node1 (cmd-91630078fda14768, cmd-a998573051984e87) for diagnosing the vbash alias issue; no evidence they were cleaned up afterward, representing minor abandoned residue. No failed/conflicting services, no unnecessary package installs, no weakened security controls; package-health/failed-services checks show clean state before and after in the lifecycle snapshots. Overall hygiene is good with only minor, low-risk residue (temp test scripts) not confirmed cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-41aa54e55bd64bf3","cmd-43614ed87cd742f4","cmd-926ca1b6948a4131","cmd-d421ddc2da10498a","cmd-8446b2b324d546a7","cmd-91630078fda14768","cmd-a998573051984e87","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor successfully configured node1 (VyOS) as a NAT gateway via Ansible, with LAN interface addressing, source NAT masquerade, and IP forwarding all verified. node2 and node3 were configured with static LAN addressing and preferred default routes, and end-to-end internet egress through node1 was directly demonstrated via ping/curl. The NAT dependency was proven through a deliberate disable/re-enable test showing connectivity loss and recovery. The implementation is fully via Ansible (inventory.ini + nat-egress.yml run through node4's run.sh) and was shown to be idempotent on a settled run. All four managed nodes were then put through the restart evidence protocol with pre-restart healthy baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and confirmed recovery of role-specific state (NAT/interface on node1, static IP/route/egress on node2/node3, and idempotent Ansible control plus reconfirmed cluster egress on node4). All material task outcomes and the independent restart protocol are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence.","limitations":["A small temporary diagnostic script (/tmp/test-script.sh and /tmp/test-script2.sh) written on node1 during troubleshooting was not confirmed removed in later evidence, though this is low-risk and does not affect NAT functionality.","Several early configuration attempts failed due to a VyOS alias-expansion quirk with 'set -e'; while these failures were diagnostic and ultimately corrected, the trace shows a period of manual (outside-playbook) configuration via direct SSH before the playbook itself was fixed to apply the same configuration idempotently.","Fixed lifecycle observations only include before_prepare (aliased as after_prepare, since prepare was disabled) and after_executor snapshots, so no independent mid-task snapshot exists to cross-check intermediate state beyond the executor's own command trace."]},"ttft_ms":2921,"type":